<!--
The whole site: a router serving the `routes/` directory, every page wrapped
in the `templates/Layout.bsx` document. The servers own the boot and the router is
their dispatch child; the router's middleware attaches beside it as component
spreads (request logging, `--help`, terminal link navigation, the layout). It
declares five servers, so `--server` picks one on load: the default `HttpServer`,
`--server=tui` the live `TuiServer`, `--server=cli` a one-shot render, and
`--server=dom` the browser, which is how the served page boots this same entry
in the tab (see `templates/Layout.bsx`):

    beet --main=examples/bsx_site/main.bsx --server=http
    cd examples/bsx_site && beet --server=tui   # or rely on discovery

There is no `main.rs`: the binary discovers this entry and declares the load live,
so the root `<CallOnReady>` calls its own action with the process request, starting
the servers the site declares the moment the entry loads. `<DefaultAppRoutes/>`
adds the default app routes (eg `/app-info`) as child routes. The `<PackageConfig/>` declaration patches the named
fields of the resource, creating it from defaults if absent, so the title and
description are set in markup with no compile-time host config.

`<ServeBlobs prefix="repo"/>` publishes this directory read-only at `/repo`, the
store the browser process reads the entry through, and `<AssetsDir>` mounts the
assets the chrome references and the wasm binary the page boots: `assets` links
to the workspace tree, so `just build-wasm-ui` is what installs the binary.
-->
<CallOnReady {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer, DomServer, CliServer)}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler, NavigateHandler, Layout{template:"Layout"})}>
	<PackageConfig title="BSX Site" description="A beet site with zero code"/>
	<Theme color={Srgba(Srgba{red:0.5, green:0.2, blue:0.9, alpha:1.0})}/>
	<TemplateDir src="templates"/>
	<Styles/>
	<DefaultAppRoutes/>
	<RoutesDir src="routes"/>
	<ServeBlobs prefix="repo"/>
	<AssetsDir src="assets" prefix="assets"/>
</Router>
</CallOnReady>
